Keep the SSE framing intact in the benchmark client's stream reader.

vllm/benchmarks/lib/endpoint_request_func.py strips each raw chunk before
handing it to StreamedResponseHandler, at all three streaming call sites. That
removes the blank line ending an SSE message, which is what the handler splits
on. Its fallback only empties the buffer when it starts with "data: ", so a
": keep-alive" comment line stays there, every later message is appended behind
it, and the buffer never starts with "data: " again. Every token of that
request is lost and the client reports

  Never received a valid chunk to calculate TTFT.This response will be marked
  as failed!

Reproduced against the shipped handler, no server needed:

  without keep-alive:  add_chunk -> ['data: {"a":1}'], ['data: {"a":2}']
  with keep-alive:     add_chunk -> [], [], []
                       buffer: ': keep-alivedata: {"a":1}data: {"a":2}'

Not stripping restores it: the comment line comes out as its own message and
the existing `if message.startswith(":"): continue` drops it, which is what the
reader was already written to do.

Only visible when a prefill outlasts the server's keep-alive interval, so
ordinary short-prompt runs never hit it. Here patches/sse-keep-alive.patch
emits the comment lines and single-user/start_qwen.sh sets the interval to 30 s,
so bench/run_benchmarks.sh --long (1x100k, 4x60k) reports zero for every timing
while the server generates normally: 89 drafts, 168 accepted tokens, acceptance
length 2.89, and meanTTFT=0.00 / TPOT=0.00 beside it.

--- a/benchmarks/lib/endpoint_request_func.py
+++ b/benchmarks/lib/endpoint_request_func.py
@@ -205,8 +205,13 @@
                 handler = StreamedResponseHandler()
 
                 async for chunk_bytes in response.content.iter_any():
-                    chunk_bytes = chunk_bytes.strip()
-                    if not chunk_bytes:
+                    # Do not strip here: it removes the blank line that ends an SSE
+                    # message, which is what StreamedResponseHandler splits on. A
+                    # ': keep-alive' comment line then stays in the buffer, which is only
+                    # emptied when it starts with 'data: ' -- after that it never does
+                    # again and every later message is lost. Only reachable when a prefill
+                    # outlasts the server's keep-alive interval.
+                    if not chunk_bytes.strip():
                         continue
 
                     messages = handler.add_chunk(chunk_bytes)
@@ -383,8 +388,13 @@
             if response.status == 200:
                 handler = StreamedResponseHandler()
                 async for chunk_bytes in response.content.iter_any():
-                    chunk_bytes = chunk_bytes.strip()
-                    if not chunk_bytes:
+                    # Do not strip here: it removes the blank line that ends an SSE
+                    # message, which is what StreamedResponseHandler splits on. A
+                    # ': keep-alive' comment line then stays in the buffer, which is only
+                    # emptied when it starts with 'data: ' -- after that it never does
+                    # again and every later message is lost. Only reachable when a prefill
+                    # outlasts the server's keep-alive interval.
+                    if not chunk_bytes.strip():
                         continue
 
                     message_strings = handler.add_chunk(chunk_bytes)
@@ -504,8 +514,13 @@
                     handler = StreamedResponseHandler()
 
